WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.919
It is time for it to come to an end.

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.269
"Chaos" is the word I think many

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.129
people in San Francisco would use to

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.389
describe the situation.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:07.799
It's homelessness problem has been

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.329
out of control for years.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.069
But COVID made it even worse.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:12.569
Is shoplifting happening every

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:12.917
single day?

00:00:12.917 --> 00:00:14.069
Every single day, any time they

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.619
want.
Residents here are mortified.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:17.265
We're beyond wit's end.

00:00:17.266 --> 00:00:19.379
It comes to an end

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.329
when we take the steps

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.399
to be more aggressive with

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.979
law enforcement and less tolerant

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.509
of all the bullshit that has

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.039
destroyed our city.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.169
On December 17th, 2021,

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:33.029
San Francisco Mayor London Breed

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.739
declared a state of emergency in the

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.479
city's Tenderloin district, which

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.949
will lead to an increased police

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.599
presence in the epicenter of the

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.189
city's growing homelessness and

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:42.509
addiction crisis.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:44.519
It was a major turnaround for Breed,

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:45.929
who, after the murder of George

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:47.849
Floyd in 2020, called for

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.379
ending the use of police in

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.359
responding to non-criminal activity.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.249
And she was roundly criticized by

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.199
progressive politicians and groups

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.119
like the Coalition on Homelessness,

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:58.949
who called the move an expansion of

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.779
strategies that have been tried and

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.639
failed that would contribute to the

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.619
instability and poor public health

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.149
outcomes of people living on the

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.039
streets. Michael Shellenberger,

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.449
author of the new book San

00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:11.489
Fransicko: Why Progressives

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.499
Ruined Cities, calls Breed's

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.629
new tough love approach a big step

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:16.769
in the right direction.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:18.689
The homelessness crisis, he says, is

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.399
actually an addiction and mental

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.529
health crisis enabled by progressive

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.509
policies that permit open air

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.279
drug scenes on public property,

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:28.169
prevent police from enforcing crimes

00:01:28.170 --> 00:01:29.729
and undermine the creation of a

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.319
functional mental health system.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.179
Reason spoke with Shellenberger, a

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:35.069
Bay Area activist best known for

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.109
his advocacy of nuclear power,

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:39.539
about his foray into social policy.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.519
His critiques of both progressive

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.319
and libertarian politics, mayor

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.209
Breed's new approach and how he

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:46.889
thinks America's big cities can

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:48.569
clean up their streets without

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:51.059
grossly violating civil liberties.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.099
So your new book is

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.499
San Fransicko: Why Progressives

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.239
Ruined Cities.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.769
First off, can we talk about the

00:01:58.770 --> 00:01:59.699
title for a second?

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.319
It's a little aggressive, no.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.359
Are you worried about scaring off

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.949
potentially persuadable people?

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:07.079
I do worry about that.

00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:08.788
I don't want to scare off anybody,

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:10.258
but I also believe in truth in

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:12.419
advertising, and the title

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:13.449
is accurate.

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:15.239
You know, the basic argument of the

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.129
book is that San Francisco and

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:19.529
other liberal cities are sick.

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.359
They have untreated

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:22.259
they have people with untreated

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:23.489
mental illness and untreated

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:24.539
addiction.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.609
Camping in parks, on

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:28.199
sidewalks, using drugs, publicly

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.969
defecating publicly.

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:31.889
It's a huge health

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:33.929
public health problem,

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.869
and I don't just mean homelessness.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:37.799
I mean the broader, you

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.809
know, urban decay

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:41.549
and including rising crime.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.619
I also think I also argue that

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.479
there's a kind of

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.699
sick way of thinking about

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.799
these problems, which is to

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.959
pathologize and to

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:53.909
view the world as and

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:56.279
to view the, you know, our system,

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:58.349
as you know, fundamentally

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.329
evil and wrong and that that

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:02.219
leads to terrible outcomes.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.929
You know, the book is not it is

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:05.519
about San Francisco, but it's also

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:07.229
really about what the subtitle says,

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:08.399
which is why progressives ruin

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.449
cities.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.699
It was a question I had and and

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.529
and still am asking, but try to

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:15.119
get to the bottom of which is why is

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.979
it that cities that ostensibly

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.409
care the most about

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.269
poor people, minorities,

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:22.439
people suffering mental illness and

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.119
addiction. Why do they treat them so

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.349
terribly?

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:26.819
What's going on is the issue of

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:28.499
money. Is that a lack of housing?

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.809
Is it a lack of rehab?

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:32.429
What's going on?

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:33.959
That's the that's the reason for the

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:34.529
book.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:35.530
I'm curious

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:38.579
about your journey

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:40.649
to this topic because

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.389
you're kind of known as the nuclear

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.419
guy. That's how I knew you.

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.639
But there is sort of a through line

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.499
here. I mean, you came

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.839
from progressive politics

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:53.249
in the environmental movement and

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:55.289
started to question some of that

00:03:55.290 --> 00:03:56.489
in terms of whether it was actually

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:58.319
achieving its goal.

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:00.689
Now you've expanded your focus to

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:02.939
urban dysfunction more generally.

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:05.429
Is there some sort of connection

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:07.769
there or did

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:08.879
were there other reasons that you

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.589
decided to expand your focus?

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:13.399
Yeah, for sure.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:14.839
And I'm still a nuclear guy, and I'm

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:15.829
still working on energy, the

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:17.119
environment, I still write about it

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.009
and I still campaigned to support

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:20.898
nuclear. In fact,

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:23.059
our nuclear work is really

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:24.709
more salient than ever because we're

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:26.539
in a global energy crisis because

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:27.889
of in part because of lack of

00:04:27.890 --> 00:04:29.839
nuclear and also the lack of natural

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:31.639
gas, which is also fuel that I

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:32.640
support.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.979
So, yeah, there is, and it took I

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:36.179
had to actually write the book to

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:37.889
see some of the connections, but for

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:39.989
me and the third book

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.429
also coming from the same publisher

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:42.430
Harper Collins,

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.659
probably next year

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.499
and there will be three, there'll

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.229
be three books Apocalypse Never, San

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:50.729
Francisco, and then the third book.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:51.899
And they're all dealing with this

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.299
issue of really

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:55.889
the threats to civilization from

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:57.119
within the civilization.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:58.619
Like, why is it that we're having

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.389
serious problems of governance?

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:01.829
Why is it that our institutions are

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:03.089
failing?

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:05.249
And you know, the most dramatic,

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.349
I think example of really

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:08.669
the progressive attack on

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:10.709
institutions is

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.809
when I was reading about the effort

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:14.939
to shut down psychiatric hospitals,

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:16.109
which had a bunch of problems and

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.099
needed it to be reformed.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:18.579
But nonetheless, they were shut down

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.609
and this kind of panic

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:22.199
without having adequate places for

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:23.939
people to go or requiring people to

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:24.959
be there.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.909
It reminded me a lot of

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:28.619
the panic of shutting down nuclear

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.839
plants and and I keep seeing

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:32.049
it over and over again.

00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:33.329
And in fact, Greta Thunberg the

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:34.679
climate activist.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.419
Her most famous line is "I don't

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:37.589
want you to have hope, I want you to

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:39.599
panic." Which I was

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:40.883
very critical of in Apocalypse

00:05:40.884 --> 00:05:43.019
Never, panic literally means

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.879
unthinking behavior and

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.049
action, which is not something we

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:47.279
should wish on our worst enemies.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:49.139
And I see myself making the

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:51.539
case for civilization.

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:53.729
And that sounds a little grandiose,

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.559
but but certainly making

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.569
the case for institutions

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:58.859
that you have to have that

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:01.409
civilization is cities

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:02.519
sort of literally what it meant.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:03.809
But those cities depend on

00:06:03.810 --> 00:06:05.039
institutions, and it means that you

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:06.449
need to have police and you need to

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:07.679
have criminal justice and you need

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:09.569
to have functioning electricity

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.759
grids and you need to have homeless

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.769
shelters. And

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:15.599
so you need all these things.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.069
And and there's something that

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:18.419
happens when kind of the radical

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:20.549
left really muscles

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:22.619
liberals and Democrats into taking

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:24.149
some pretty radical actions, and it

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:25.409
certainly happened on the right too.

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:26.519
I'm not suggesting it doesn't.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.139
But but I see I see that as the

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:29.789
through line in the work,

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:31.469
The perspective that you bring to

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.629
this as a former activist

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:36.209
involved in progressive causes.

00:06:36.210 --> 00:06:38.159
I have to say it's oddly relatable

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.169
to me because progressive

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:42.119
politics and libertarian politics

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:43.949
overlap in many of these very

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:46.079
areas: drug legalization,

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.149
criminal justice reform,

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:49.619
the rights of those with mental

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:50.909
illness.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:52.979
Yet as a recently

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:54.899
former Californian who has

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.789
seen and documented and

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:58.649
experienced a lot of

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:00.059
the tragedy unfolding on the

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:01.619
streets, I've had to personally

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.479
think very deeply about

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:05.339
how some of these policies have been

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:07.169
implemented and their real

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:08.789
world effects.

00:07:08.790 --> 00:07:10.439
You know, I still want to see major

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.539
changes in laws and sentencing

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.519
across the country that maximize

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:15.899
personal liberty.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.029
But my general sense and

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.039
it was bolstered by

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:21.929
a lot of what you dug

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:23.969
into in the book here, is

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:26.519
that the way it's been implemented

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.349
in practice

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:30.239
has emphasized the personal

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:32.399
liberty side of the equation

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:34.349
while ignoring the personal

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:37.019
responsibilities part and

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:38.039
for a libertarian.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.079
Those things are bound

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.609
together.

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.769
What is your big

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:45.719
picture diagnosis of what's gone

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:47.909
wrong in California cities?

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:49.409
Yeah, thank you for that and I

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:50.459
totally agree with you.

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:52.379
I mean, I love my freedom and I'm in

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:53.969
San Francisco. I'm in the I'm in

00:07:53.970 --> 00:07:55.259
Berkeley, which is across the bay

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:56.129
from San Francisco. I'm in

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:57.989
California because I love the

00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:00.479
cultural libertarianism here.

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:03.299
I love the fact that we

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:04.739
really reject a lot of the

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:06.569
traditional statist hierarchy of

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:08.489
the East Coast around where you went

00:08:08.490 --> 00:08:10.889
to school and working in big,

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:12.509
you know, companies and stuff like

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:14.699
that. I love the entrepreneurialism.

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:16.169
I just think that's the most special

00:08:16.170 --> 00:08:17.969
part of the of the of the left

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:18.989
coast.

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:20.129
So I totally agree.

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:21.929
I also care a lot about people.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.149
I mean, I think the libertarians

00:08:24.150 --> 00:08:25.589
lay down people is that they care

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:26.549
about freedom more.

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:27.899
And the trip that progressives lay

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.339
on people is that they care more.

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:30.779
They're more compassionate and

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:32.158
there's probably some truth to it.

00:08:32.159 --> 00:08:33.329
But I think the truth is that I

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:34.649
think a lot of us love our freedom

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:35.879
and a lot of us care a lot about

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:37.649
other people. And we're looking for,

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:38.999
particularly now, I think, some

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.949
practical ways to solve

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:42.538
these problems.

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:44.369
So I mean, I view the problem

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:46.409
out here. I view there's

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:47.369
a lot of things going on.

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:48.569
But if you kind of take the

00:08:48.570 --> 00:08:50.399
so-called homeless problem,

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:51.929
I believe that is fundamentally a

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:53.429
problem of untreated mental illness

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:54.839
and drug addiction.

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.459
Drug addiction, I believe, is a form

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:57.269
of mental illness.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.189
I also think I agree that it's often

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:01.379
self-inflicted and sometimes

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:02.669
it comes from trauma, sometimes it

00:09:02.670 --> 00:09:04.529
comes from undiagnosed depression or

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:07.049
anxiety, or even more severely

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:08.099
schizophrenia.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:09.269
But sometimes it just comes from

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:10.229
partying too much.

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:12.119
I mean, addiction is just something

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:14.459
that afflicts a bunch of people,

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:16.409
and it's untreated.

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:18.239
I agree, though, that we don't have

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:19.589
a functioning psychiatric system

00:09:19.590 --> 00:09:20.759
that really helps people to get

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:22.469
themselves like a lot of people that

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:23.819
are addicted to hard drugs might

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:25.079
have just done fine with an

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:26.939
antidepressant and some cognitive

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:29.789
behavioral therapy and exercise,

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:32.009
which works for almost everybody.

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:33.979
So. We don't we don't have that,

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:35.119
and that's the the traditional

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:36.289
progressive criticisms, we don't

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:38.269
have that, but we do need that.

00:09:38.270 --> 00:09:39.979
And then the other issue is just, of

00:09:39.980 --> 00:09:41.539
course, that why are there so many

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:42.709
people on the streets in San

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:44.269
Francisco and California?

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:45.689
It's because we let them.

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:46.909
It's there's a myth that it's

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:49.009
because of the weather.

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:50.869
Certainly in freezing places

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:52.099
like Chicago, it's hard to be on the

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:53.509
streets year-round.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:55.129
But there's other places like Miami,

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:56.839
Miami, which are warm, which don't

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:58.459
have the same problems that we have.

00:09:58.460 --> 00:09:59.689
Or least they did, and then they

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:00.829
fixed them.

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:01.639
And I think there's been some

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:03.199
backsliding, but nonetheless has had

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:05.479
much better outcomes than we have.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:07.159
So it's sometimes it's easier to

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:08.809
describe what the solution is and

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:10.009
then work through why we're not

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:11.599
doing it. And the solution is

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:14.029
basically a universal shelter.

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:15.469
I think that the Supreme Court has

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:17.129
established it, but even regardless,

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:19.009
the Supreme Court, ethically,

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:20.659
we should one that everybody should

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:22.519
have a safe and clean place to

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:23.389
sleep.

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:25.009
It should not be so nice that it

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:26.389
attracts people who want to stay

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:28.649
there, but that should be universal.

00:10:28.650 --> 00:10:29.929
That's not the policy we have.

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:31.849
We have a Housing First policy

00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:33.529
rather than a shelter first policy

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:35.449
under this utopian idea that we can

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:36.709
just provide everybody who wants

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:38.119
their own apartment in San Francisco

00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:39.859
or Venice Beach with their own

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:42.319
apartment. It's obviously wrong.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:44.299
Just geographically, you can't do

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:46.039
it, but financially you can't do it,

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:47.539
and it creates a terrible incentive

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:49.249
for people to become homeless.

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:51.229
We need to enforce laws,

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:53.209
including misdemeanors, including

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:54.679
against a public campaign, including

00:10:54.680 --> 00:10:56.299
against public defecation and public

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:57.619
drug use.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:10:59.239
Those are cries for help from

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:00.889
people, and when they break those

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:02.329
laws, they should be arrested,

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:03.799
brought before a judge and given the

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:05.599
opportunity to have rehab or

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:07.909
psychiatric care rather than

00:11:07.910 --> 00:11:09.019
jail or prison.

00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:10.489
There's nothing super extraordinary

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:11.509
about where I come to

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:13.399
philosophically, but I do

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:15.019
sort of point out that the core

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:17.089
American value has been freedom

00:11:17.090 --> 00:11:18.979
ever since our founding, and that's

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:20.389
a beautiful value and wish to

00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:21.649
continue to have that value.

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:24.019
But it does require responsibility

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:25.909
at the individual level, but

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:27.859
also at the societal level

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:29.629
and really that we need to balance

00:11:29.630 --> 00:11:31.669
those two values responsibility

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:33.079
and freedom.

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:34.999
I know from reading your book

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:36.829
that you don't want to go back

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:38.929
to the bad old days of

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:41.059
insane asylums and mass

00:11:41.060 --> 00:11:42.949
incarceration, total

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:44.809
drug war, but

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:46.759
also the current trajectory

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:49.069
in these cities is clearly

00:11:49.070 --> 00:11:51.019
not right, either.

00:11:51.020 --> 00:11:53.359
What is the middle path forward

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:55.969
from here? The synthesis?

00:11:55.970 --> 00:11:57.799
And how do you think that those of

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:00.079
us who are legitimately concerned

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:01.639
about these issues, like personal

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:03.559
liberty and autonomy and

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:05.629
not bringing in heavy handed

00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:07.519
law enforcement to

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:09.799
bother people for, you know,

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:12.259
sitting on a park bench with

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:14.599
their stuff or something

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:16.489
can be part of a

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:18.349
productive coalition to get

00:12:18.350 --> 00:12:20.299
America's cities

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:23.089
back on track for the 21st century.

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:25.079
Yeah, well, it's a great question.

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:26.959
I sort of boiled it down to the

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:28.420
three key Ps,

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:31.789
so we don't want overincarceration.

00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:33.259
That means that we need to rely more

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:36.229
heavily on good policing,

00:12:36.230 --> 00:12:38.599
on psychiatry and

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:39.589
probation.

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:40.819
And I would just include in

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:43.069
psychiatry, rehab

00:12:43.070 --> 00:12:44.449
and addiction care.

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:45.559
And I would just add to that you

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:48.379
also need some kind of caseworker.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:50.989
You need some empowered caseworkers

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:52.489
or assertive case management is

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:54.469
what's called to bridge

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:56.359
those things. So, you

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:57.539
know, one of the big arguments for

00:12:57.540 --> 00:12:59.479
defunding the cops as we say

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:00.559
"Well, the cops shouldn't be

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:02.179
responding to mental health calls.

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:03.649
We should have social workers do

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:05.359
that. Well, it sounds good.

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:07.219
But actually less than 10 percent

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:09.409
of those mental health calls

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:11.389
are safe enough for a social worker

00:13:11.390 --> 00:13:12.619
alone. I mean, a lot of those mental

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:14.269
health calls are really scary and

00:13:14.270 --> 00:13:15.859
involve people engaged in violent

00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:17.179
activities.

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:19.099
And so really, what you want is

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:21.049
to you want more

00:13:21.050 --> 00:13:23.029
soft cops and more

00:13:23.030 --> 00:13:25.069
hard social workers and you want

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:26.749
them working together.

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:29.359
And it sounds very kumbaya,

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:31.219
and in some extent it is, but it's

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:33.049
not easy, and it does take

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:35.059
the police department's changing.

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:36.109
And then that's why I think you do

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:37.699
need something like cal psych.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:39.679
So homelessness is

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:41.719
the kind of umbrella issue

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:43.789
under which many other sub

00:13:43.790 --> 00:13:45.289
categories fall.

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:47.149
And you mentioned mental

00:13:47.150 --> 00:13:48.919
illness, drug use, and we'll get

00:13:48.920 --> 00:13:50.539
into those a little bit more in a

00:13:50.540 --> 00:13:52.819
second. But there's also

00:13:52.820 --> 00:13:54.649
the issue of housing, which

00:13:54.650 --> 00:13:56.359
I would say in the book you very

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:58.579
much downplay and say,

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:00.289
you know, housing prices really are

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:02.329
not a major driver of

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:04.129
homelessness.

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:06.349
I mean, there is some empirical

00:14:06.350 --> 00:14:07.789
research out there that at least

00:14:07.790 --> 00:14:10.759
shows correlations between

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:13.099
local rents, local

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:16.159
housing prices and,

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:17.509
you know, incomes and rates of

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:18.679
homelessness.

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:20.539
So first, just taking

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:22.519
the housing plank here,

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:24.169
isn't it at least plausible to you

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:26.059
that that some subset of

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:27.949
the population here are

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:29.809
people who maybe were right on the

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:31.270
margins, they got displaced.

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:35.029
Being out of your home

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:37.159
can exacerbate various

00:14:37.160 --> 00:14:38.629
underlying mental health conditions,

00:14:38.630 --> 00:14:40.519
and so is making the

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:42.499
problem worse.

00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:44.449
Is that a plausible story

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:45.739
to you?

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:47.659
Those camps that you see,

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:49.269
the so-called homeless encampments,

00:14:49.270 --> 00:14:51.139
it's just a 100 percent addiction

00:14:51.140 --> 00:14:52.489
and mental illness.

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:53.689
I'm not saying there's not somebody

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:55.309
there who has been off drugs for a

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:56.569
little bit or suffering mental

00:14:56.570 --> 00:14:59.269
illness, but there's some underlying

00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:01.189
sickness there that is leading them

00:15:01.190 --> 00:15:02.190
to be there.

00:15:02.950 --> 00:15:03.950
There are then

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:06.819
now now could housing, could higher

00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:08.829
housing prices

00:15:08.830 --> 00:15:10.209
worsen that situation?

00:15:10.210 --> 00:15:12.369
Absolutely. I mean, so for example,

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:14.229
the classic picture is

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:15.819
somebody becomes addicted to, let's

00:15:15.820 --> 00:15:18.009
say, you know, somebody a

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:19.509
20 year old is addicted to hard

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:20.769
drugs. He's still living with his

00:15:20.770 --> 00:15:21.609
parents.

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:23.349
He stops working.

00:15:23.350 --> 00:15:25.209
He starts stealing from

00:15:25.210 --> 00:15:27.039
his parents and lying

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:29.019
to them. They try to get him into

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:30.189
rehab.

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:31.629
Then maybe he goes, It doesn't work,

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:33.099
doesn't stick. Finally, they just

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:35.469
cut him off and they kick him out.

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:37.299
He then goes and lives in a homeless

00:15:37.300 --> 00:15:38.679
encampment on the street.

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:42.099
I think that that moment of kicking

00:15:42.100 --> 00:15:43.959
somebody out probably occurs

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:45.909
much more quickly

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:48.129
and maybe occurs when you're living

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:49.899
in a much more crowded conditions

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:51.729
and much more expensive rent

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:53.679
areas. So I can totally see

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:55.509
that. The difference, though, is

00:15:55.510 --> 00:15:57.789
that do you allow

00:15:57.790 --> 00:15:59.859
the open public campaign?

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:01.119
So for example, if it were just a

00:16:01.120 --> 00:16:03.159
function of high rents,

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:04.569
then we would see homeless

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:06.609
encampments in Beverly Hills

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:08.679
and in really nice neighborhoods.

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:09.699
But they're not. They're always in

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:11.359
the same places near train stations,

00:16:11.360 --> 00:16:13.029
near public transport downtowns,

00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:16.119
in poor neighborhoods,

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:18.459
often minority communities.

00:16:18.460 --> 00:16:20.319
And so, you know, ultimately, I also

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:21.639
allude to this issue of the magnet

00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:22.629
effect. There's always, you know,

00:16:22.630 --> 00:16:24.009
policymakers are always saying that

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:25.989
because you offer social services

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:27.939
or free housing or food and shelter

00:16:27.940 --> 00:16:29.169
that it attracts homeless people

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:30.579
from around the country.

00:16:30.580 --> 00:16:31.959
I definitely found a lot of evidence

00:16:31.960 --> 00:16:32.799
for it.

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:33.999
But at the end of the day, I was

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:35.769
like, people are attracted to the

00:16:35.770 --> 00:16:37.929
open drug scene in San Francisco

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:39.669
and in Los Angeles and there in

00:16:39.670 --> 00:16:41.559
there in the open drug scene

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:43.599
is allowed to exist

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:45.369
because progressive activists,

00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:47.079
really radical left activists, but

00:16:47.080 --> 00:16:48.879
certainly after the rebranding of

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:50.409
the 1990s occurred.

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:52.389
Progressive activists have

00:16:52.390 --> 00:16:55.029
said that it's immoral to

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:56.949
disallow public camping, that

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:58.299
it's criminalizing poverty and

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:00.099
homelessness, and that it must be

00:17:00.100 --> 00:17:01.929
allowed and that only things can

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:03.849
be given and nothing, nothing taken.

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:05.709
So I don't want to suggest there's

00:17:05.710 --> 00:17:07.539
no role for high rents, but in

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:08.949
some of the most interesting studies

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:09.759
where they were a site where

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:11.229
scholars looked at this.

00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:12.249
You're right, they did find a

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:13.389
correlation.

00:17:13.390 --> 00:17:15.249
But they also noted that

00:17:15.250 --> 00:17:16.838
in the places where you have high

00:17:16.839 --> 00:17:18.939
amounts of unsheltered homelessness

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:21.338
tend to be very progressive cities.

00:17:21.339 --> 00:17:22.389
They didn't quite use the language,

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:23.709
but cities where they had different

00:17:23.710 --> 00:17:25.539
social values.

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:27.909
So, you know, people's attitudes

00:17:27.910 --> 00:17:29.919
towards the homeless or towards the

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:31.539
street addicts are different in

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:33.519
Houston and Dallas than they are

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:35.799
in San Francisco and Seattle.

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:38.109
There's this ideology

00:17:38.110 --> 00:17:39.909
called Housing First, which you

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:41.769
mentioned earlier, and

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:44.199
it's really the operating assumption

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:47.019
of most homelessness policy

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:48.819
in all these cities, even at the

00:17:48.820 --> 00:17:50.769
national level, at all the

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:53.769
large nonprofits, which is that

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:55.779
just get people under

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:57.669
a roof in their own

00:17:57.670 --> 00:17:59.709
private space, and that

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:01.629
will begin to fix the problem

00:18:01.630 --> 00:18:02.979
and turn things around.

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:04.899
And it sounds very clean

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:06.309
and intuitive,

00:18:07.510 --> 00:18:09.729
and there's a certain simplicity

00:18:09.730 --> 00:18:12.119
to it that's very appealing.

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:14.339
From covering it in L.A., I

00:18:14.340 --> 00:18:16.259
know that it's not workable,

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:18.599
at least in a city like Los Angeles,

00:18:18.600 --> 00:18:20.939
where the economic reality

00:18:20.940 --> 00:18:22.829
of building these units has caused

00:18:22.830 --> 00:18:24.659
them to go up, you know, above half

00:18:24.660 --> 00:18:26.459
a million dollars per unit in some

00:18:26.460 --> 00:18:28.679
cases. Why doesn't housing

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:30.329
first solve homelessness?

00:18:30.330 --> 00:18:31.859
What's not working here?

00:18:31.860 --> 00:18:33.399
The basic reason is that if you go

00:18:33.400 --> 00:18:34.829
get people with untreated mental

00:18:34.830 --> 00:18:36.089
illness and addiction in their own

00:18:36.090 --> 00:18:38.039
apartments and you don't deal with

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:39.149
the untreated mental illness or

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:41.039
addiction, they actually do end

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:42.539
up back on the street, often very

00:18:42.540 --> 00:18:43.469
quickly.

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:44.909
We now know from a big Harvard study

00:18:44.910 --> 00:18:46.469
that was done over 12 years that

00:18:46.470 --> 00:18:47.399
there was no better.

00:18:47.400 --> 00:18:49.409
There was no better outcomes for

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:50.669
people that had a housing first, the

00:18:50.670 --> 00:18:52.799
non housing first, even on their own

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:55.349
metric of keeping people in housing.

00:18:55.350 --> 00:18:57.029
That is the only metric that they've

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:58.679
been able to boast of success, but

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:00.239
we have seen no evidence.

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:02.549
The National Academies of Science,

00:19:02.550 --> 00:19:04.739
which is our premier research body,

00:19:04.740 --> 00:19:07.049
did a review of the science.

00:19:07.050 --> 00:19:08.729
I think a couple of years ago, maybe

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:10.589
twenty eighteen, and they found that

00:19:10.590 --> 00:19:12.479
Housing First did not have any

00:19:12.480 --> 00:19:13.679
improved outcomes and that there was

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:15.569
some evidence that it had

00:19:15.570 --> 00:19:17.489
worse outcomes because

00:19:17.490 --> 00:19:18.689
they didn't deal with the underlying

00:19:18.690 --> 00:19:19.889
problem of addiction and mental

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:22.379
illness there.

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:25.229
The response, just to be fair to my

00:19:25.230 --> 00:19:27.119
opponents on this, their response

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:28.829
is they said, "Well, we never said

00:19:28.830 --> 00:19:29.939
that you wouldn't also have

00:19:29.940 --> 00:19:32.209
services." So I tell a story

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:34.169
in the book about my

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:36.449
Dutch character is a social worker

00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:38.309
and how he dealt with a man with

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:39.179
schizophrenia.

00:19:39.180 --> 00:19:41.099
Very serious schizophrenia.

00:19:41.100 --> 00:19:42.839
The original housing first idea, by

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:44.729
the way, came from you using

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:46.019
that strategy with people with

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:48.539
mental illness, not addiction.

00:19:48.540 --> 00:19:49.829
And there was a lot of similarities,

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:51.389
but there's also a lot of
differences,

00:19:51.390 --> 00:19:52.619
particularly with things like

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:54.359
schizophrenia and drug addiction.

00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:55.979
But my

00:19:57.150 --> 00:19:58.999
character, his name is Renee.

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:01.049
He he tells a story of basically

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:02.369
kind of muscling.

00:20:02.370 --> 00:20:04.019
A friend of the family who had

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:06.089
schizophrenia into

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:07.679
first some shelters, then into

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:08.909
psychiatric hospitals a couple of

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:10.799
times, and then he finally got

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:11.909
them on good meds.

00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:13.799
And the guy now has his own job, his

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:16.739
own apartment, his own car.

00:20:16.740 --> 00:20:18.779
And for schizophrenia, that's just

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:20.819
an extraordinarily positive outcome.

00:20:20.820 --> 00:20:22.409
I mean, he tells a story about a guy

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:24.749
still has hallucinations

00:20:24.750 --> 00:20:25.739
and delusions. He thought people

00:20:25.740 --> 00:20:26.639
were staring at him through his

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:28.529
window, and Renee's like, just

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:29.489
close the curtains.

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:31.319
You know, that's a

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:32.939
beautiful outcome.

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:35.229
And I think that is what they're

00:20:35.230 --> 00:20:37.229
the best of the housing first people

00:20:37.230 --> 00:20:38.879
wanted. And they stressed, they said

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:40.499
you'd have services in that case

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:42.359
would be Renee or somebody

00:20:42.360 --> 00:20:44.129
to be playing that role.

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:45.089
But that's just not what's been

00:20:45.090 --> 00:20:46.889
occurring. And some of that isn't

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:48.119
the fault of progressives.

00:20:48.120 --> 00:20:49.019
It's because we don't have a

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:50.069
functioning psychiatric and

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:51.659
addiction care services.

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:53.509
But a lot of it is also victim

00:20:53.510 --> 00:20:55.349
ideology, which is a

00:20:55.350 --> 00:20:57.239
weirdly libertarian

00:20:57.240 --> 00:20:58.589
part of it. You know, which is sort

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:00.869
of like just it's liberal,

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:02.519
left libertarian, a kind of

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:04.109
anarchist libertarian, which is sort

00:21:04.110 --> 00:21:06.749
of like you, they're victims

00:21:06.750 --> 00:21:08.609
of trauma and racism and poverty

00:21:08.610 --> 00:21:10.319
and whatever and addiction and

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:11.789
almost give them the means.

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:13.229
You should give them things like

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:15.149
their own apartment and offer them

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:17.039
help, but don't require

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:18.899
it. Don't mandate it, because that

00:21:18.900 --> 00:21:20.039
would be an extension of the

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:21.209
victimization.

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:22.289
And that's where everything goes

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:24.779
wrong. I mean, addiction,

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:26.409
particularly focus on addiction for

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:28.709
a second, but even schizophrenia,

00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:30.269
there is some amount of coercion

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:31.949
that's usually required for people

00:21:31.950 --> 00:21:33.029
to quit their addiction.

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:34.919
People do need to be arrested or in

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:35.849
Portugal.

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:37.949
They do these interventions

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:39.569
with a kind of mix of family

00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:41.279
members, social workers, you know,

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:43.199
government officials and cops.

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:45.089
But still, it's pressure on

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:46.259
people and we know this about

00:21:46.260 --> 00:21:47.519
addiction. We have a whole reality

00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:49.799
TV show called Intervention.

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:51.689
That shouldn't be so surprising, but

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:52.829
I think there's that's where the

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:55.289
dogma has interrupted

00:21:55.290 --> 00:21:57.269
the proper treatment of people with

00:21:57.270 --> 00:21:58.679
addiction and mental illnesses and

00:21:58.680 --> 00:22:00.089
actually requiring some amount of

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:01.289
pressure or coercion.

00:22:01.290 --> 00:22:02.879
There's this kind of zero sum game

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:05.399
and competing for funds between

00:22:05.400 --> 00:22:07.259
shelters and permanent

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:08.519
supportive housing.

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:10.559
You know, for every one person

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:12.899
permanent supportive housing unit,

00:22:12.900 --> 00:22:14.609
you could build, you know, tons of

00:22:14.610 --> 00:22:16.979
beds in a shelter,

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:18.239
you know, in addition to the

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:20.369
economics of scale there,

00:22:20.370 --> 00:22:22.289
that with the shelter model,

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:25.319
you can still implement this

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:27.479
sort of reward system where

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:29.039
you can work your way up from the

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:30.899
shelter to, you know, a private

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:33.599
room to your own place.

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:36.269
Is that the appeal of shelters?

00:22:36.270 --> 00:22:38.219
There is still a role for, I think,

00:22:38.220 --> 00:22:39.276
publicly subsidized housing, again

00:22:39.277 --> 00:22:40.277
that's something we can talk about

00:22:41.490 --> 00:22:43.109
in terms of the libertarian concerns

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:44.429
about it. I think it should be

00:22:44.430 --> 00:22:45.569
limited.

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:46.679
I think the goal should be

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:48.449
independence, which I think

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:49.769
everybody should share for people to

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:50.939
the extent that that's possible.

00:22:50.940 --> 00:22:52.499
I mean, truly disabled people,

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:54.209
obviously, that's not possible.

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:56.369
And so one of the things that

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:58.289
I would see the social workers do

00:22:58.290 --> 00:22:59.549
in the Netherlands is they would

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:01.679
offer people their own private room

00:23:01.680 --> 00:23:03.719
as an incentive or eventually

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:05.039
maybe their own apartment if you can

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:06.179
afford it or if you have that.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:07.769
But even their own room is a really

00:23:07.770 --> 00:23:09.269
good incentive for people because

00:23:09.270 --> 00:23:11.209
people don't like sharing rooms

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:12.210
and people don't like congregation
spaces

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:14.839
everybody is the same in that way.

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:16.759
And so that housing

00:23:16.760 --> 00:23:18.679
earned approach we had there was big

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:20.509
studies done on this in

00:23:20.510 --> 00:23:22.849
Birmingham, Alabama, actually

00:23:22.850 --> 00:23:24.709
clean on crack and they would find

00:23:24.710 --> 00:23:26.689
that crack addicts who

00:23:26.690 --> 00:23:28.549
were given their own

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:30.589
apartment as a reward for

00:23:30.590 --> 00:23:32.419
passing a drug test and being off

00:23:32.420 --> 00:23:34.339
cocaine did very

00:23:34.340 --> 00:23:35.749
well. It actually, I mean, it's

00:23:35.750 --> 00:23:37.129
still, you know, it's not perfect.

00:23:37.130 --> 00:23:38.659
But but it did much better than the

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:40.549
Housing First, folks, when

00:23:40.550 --> 00:23:42.109
they had some sort of an incentive

00:23:42.110 --> 00:23:43.489
to change their behavior.

00:23:43.490 --> 00:23:45.379
I want to talk to you about

00:23:45.380 --> 00:23:47.509
the drug war, which I still see

00:23:47.510 --> 00:23:49.699
as a giant failure and think

00:23:49.700 --> 00:23:51.469
that decriminalization and

00:23:51.470 --> 00:23:53.329
eventually legalization is the right

00:23:53.330 --> 00:23:55.279
path forward to alleviate

00:23:55.280 --> 00:23:56.719
a lot of the suffering we see on the

00:23:56.720 --> 00:23:58.849
streets and sidewalks, though

00:23:58.850 --> 00:24:01.159
maybe done in a perhaps more careful

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:02.989
way than it's been done in some of

00:24:02.990 --> 00:24:03.889
these cities.

00:24:03.890 --> 00:24:05.149
You say in the book that you were

00:24:05.150 --> 00:24:07.159
once a critic of the drug war.

00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:09.199
Do you still think it's a good idea?

00:24:09.200 --> 00:24:10.669
You know, given what we know about

00:24:10.670 --> 00:24:12.559
the effects of the black market,

00:24:12.560 --> 00:24:14.059
people having their lives ruined

00:24:14.060 --> 00:24:16.519
with jail or prison time

00:24:16.520 --> 00:24:19.339
is keeping drugs

00:24:19.340 --> 00:24:21.169
criminalized a viable path

00:24:21.170 --> 00:24:22.339
forward.

00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:24.049
So I mean, I think if you kind of go

00:24:24.050 --> 00:24:26.029
to the drug war, succeed or fail,

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:27.289
well, on the one hand, it's

00:24:27.290 --> 00:24:29.479
obviously spectacularly failed

00:24:29.480 --> 00:24:31.339
on every level because we

00:24:31.340 --> 00:24:33.259
have 100,000 people that died from

00:24:33.260 --> 00:24:34.459
drugs last year.

00:24:34.460 --> 00:24:36.799
That's an increase from 17,000

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:37.909
in the year 2,000.

00:24:37.910 --> 00:24:39.739
I mean, it's just the numbers are

00:24:39.740 --> 00:24:41.209
mind blowing.

00:24:41.210 --> 00:24:42.469
You know, that's three times more

00:24:42.470 --> 00:24:43.759
people dying of drugs than car

00:24:43.760 --> 00:24:45.619
accidents, five times more dying

00:24:45.620 --> 00:24:47.119
than from a homicides.

00:24:47.120 --> 00:24:48.499
So number one cause of death right

00:24:48.500 --> 00:24:50.509
now for people between the ages

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:51.919
of 18 and 45.

00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:54.049
So, you know, these are terrible

00:24:54.050 --> 00:24:55.050
numbers.

00:24:55.880 --> 00:24:57.049
I want to be careful, though,

00:24:57.050 --> 00:24:58.939
because I think that I think

00:24:58.940 --> 00:25:00.139
that I mean, what I'll say is, I

00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:02.089
think we are we are I think we are

00:25:02.090 --> 00:25:03.799
evolving and I hope that San

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:05.179
Fransicko is a part of it.

00:25:05.180 --> 00:25:06.679
I believe it is a part of the

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:08.569
evolution of our thinking,

00:25:08.570 --> 00:25:10.999
which had traditionally been

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:12.829
just decriminalized drugs

00:25:12.830 --> 00:25:14.939
or your or a prosecuter

00:25:14.940 --> 00:25:16.189
and you want to lock them up.

00:25:16.190 --> 00:25:18.109
I think there is a third way, and

00:25:18.110 --> 00:25:19.110
that's I. hesitate to use that word
because

00:25:20.420 --> 00:25:22.789
it's been so kind of contaminated,

00:25:22.790 --> 00:25:24.139
but basically, I do think that

00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:26.209
there's a better way, which is much

00:25:26.210 --> 00:25:28.279
more similar to the Dutch and

00:25:28.280 --> 00:25:30.169
the Portuguese model, although the

00:25:30.170 --> 00:25:32.329
Portuguese have much tighter family

00:25:32.330 --> 00:25:33.769
and community networks, so it's a

00:25:33.770 --> 00:25:35.029
little bit harder to replicate.

00:25:35.030 --> 00:25:37.429
But nonetheless the Dutch model,

00:25:37.430 --> 00:25:39.559
they are putting pressure on addicts

00:25:39.560 --> 00:25:40.939
to quit.

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:43.009
At the same time, if you're using

00:25:43.010 --> 00:25:45.409
heroin, cocaine in the privacy

00:25:45.410 --> 00:25:46.849
of your own home and you're not

00:25:46.850 --> 00:25:48.559
camping on the streets.

00:25:48.560 --> 00:25:50.389
There's no they're

00:25:50.390 --> 00:25:51.889
not going to go after you and

00:25:51.890 --> 00:25:52.909
they're really not going in after

00:25:52.910 --> 00:25:55.009
you hear either.

00:25:55.010 --> 00:25:57.889
And in terms of drug dealing,

00:25:57.890 --> 00:25:59.989
drug dealing continues

00:25:59.990 --> 00:26:01.549
in the Netherlands and it continues

00:26:01.550 --> 00:26:03.109
all over the world.

00:26:03.110 --> 00:26:04.819
And I don't think you can get rid of

00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:06.709
it. Or I would say, I don't think

00:26:06.710 --> 00:26:08.629
you could get rid of drug dealing or

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:10.249
drug use without a severe

00:26:10.250 --> 00:26:12.049
curtailment of our civil liberties,

00:26:12.050 --> 00:26:13.939
which nobody, not even

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:15.559
the most fascist really in our

00:26:15.560 --> 00:26:17.509
society would support.

00:26:17.510 --> 00:26:19.729
But you can disallow

00:26:19.730 --> 00:26:21.709
open drug scenes, which

00:26:21.710 --> 00:26:23.599
is the homeless encampments.

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:25.339
You can disallow open air drug

00:26:25.340 --> 00:26:26.329
dealing.

00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:27.769
It's hard, but it is.

00:26:27.770 --> 00:26:29.269
We do know how to do it, which is

00:26:29.270 --> 00:26:31.159
that you basically suppress

00:26:31.160 --> 00:26:33.229
any, you know, if you see one

00:26:33.230 --> 00:26:35.089
dealer and one addict making

00:26:35.090 --> 00:26:36.139
a sale on the street, it's not a

00:26:36.140 --> 00:26:37.399
priority for police.

00:26:37.400 --> 00:26:39.979
But once it becomes known,

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:41.389
and part of the problem is that like

00:26:41.390 --> 00:26:43.219
addicts, if you live in Cleveland,

00:26:43.220 --> 00:26:44.689
like you know that San Francisco is

00:26:44.690 --> 00:26:46.249
a place that you can go to and use

00:26:46.250 --> 00:26:48.049
heroin openly and freely on a low

00:26:48.050 --> 00:26:49.159
cost.

00:26:49.160 --> 00:26:50.989
And so when you shut that open-air

00:26:50.990 --> 00:26:52.819
drug scene down an open

00:26:52.820 --> 00:26:54.589
drug dealing down, it massively

00:26:54.590 --> 00:26:55.849
improves the security of the

00:26:55.850 --> 00:26:56.719
neighborhoods.

00:26:56.720 --> 00:26:58.669
I mean, I think I can get behind the

00:26:58.670 --> 00:27:01.339
idea of cities

00:27:01.340 --> 00:27:03.169
prohibiting these open

00:27:03.170 --> 00:27:05.089
air drug scenes on public property.

00:27:05.090 --> 00:27:07.129
I think you could make that case on

00:27:07.130 --> 00:27:08.130
libertarian grounds even.

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:12.139
Where it gets dicey

00:27:12.140 --> 00:27:13.969
is with this issue of

00:27:13.970 --> 00:27:15.799
personal consumption,

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:18.049
even, you know, selling to people

00:27:18.050 --> 00:27:20.059
within the privacy of your own

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:22.879
home or some private venue.

00:27:22.880 --> 00:27:24.139
Is that somewhere?

00:27:24.140 --> 00:27:26.029
And Portugal, for

00:27:26.030 --> 00:27:28.009
the record, actually, they

00:27:28.010 --> 00:27:30.139
they will arrest you just for

00:27:30.140 --> 00:27:31.999
doing drugs and bring you in front

00:27:32.000 --> 00:27:33.829
of a drug court and then kind

00:27:33.830 --> 00:27:36.499
of force you into rehab.

00:27:36.500 --> 00:27:38.329
But are you willing to, you know,

00:27:38.330 --> 00:27:40.009
kind of draw a bright line there and

00:27:40.010 --> 00:27:42.259
say, you know, let's just focus

00:27:42.260 --> 00:27:44.359
on the open air drug scenes

00:27:44.360 --> 00:27:46.339
and not going after

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:47.539
casual drug users?

00:27:49.580 --> 00:27:51.109
Yeah, absolutely, I'm very

00:27:51.110 --> 00:27:52.519
comfortable with that.

00:27:52.520 --> 00:27:54.799
And I think actually being explicit

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:56.989
about it and creating some societal

00:27:56.990 --> 00:27:59.239
consensus is an important

00:27:59.240 --> 00:28:00.599
step in solving this problem.

00:28:00.600 --> 00:28:02.269
In fact, it's the first step, in

00:28:02.270 --> 00:28:03.829
fact, that study I mentioned about

00:28:03.830 --> 00:28:05.779
saying the open drug scenes they

00:28:05.780 --> 00:28:07.849
actually created like a like a list

00:28:07.850 --> 00:28:09.949
of things that all the studies did

00:28:09.950 --> 00:28:11.689
in the first one is established in

00:28:11.690 --> 00:28:13.249
the social, societal and political

00:28:13.250 --> 00:28:14.869
consensus. And they all said he did

00:28:14.870 --> 00:28:16.219
that because if you don't do that,

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:17.209
forget about it.

00:28:17.210 --> 00:28:18.979
So, yeah, absolutely.

00:28:18.980 --> 00:28:20.869
I still I am very

00:28:20.870 --> 00:28:22.489
uncomfortable with the government

00:28:22.490 --> 00:28:23.869
getting involved in selling these

00:28:23.870 --> 00:28:25.369
drugs or selling them from

00:28:25.370 --> 00:28:27.229
pharmacies. I do still think

00:28:27.230 --> 00:28:28.230
they should be.

00:28:30.680 --> 00:28:31.999
I think I think it's okay to

00:28:32.000 --> 00:28:34.159
decriminalize decriminalization

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:35.629
is different than legalization.

00:28:35.630 --> 00:28:36.679
Yes.

00:28:36.680 --> 00:28:38.569
And so I think it

00:28:38.570 --> 00:28:40.129
should be hard to get those

00:28:40.130 --> 00:28:41.197
substances. Not easy.

00:28:41.198 --> 00:28:43.519
But I'm sure you are aware

00:28:43.520 --> 00:28:45.229
of the research.

00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:46.759
You know, we've had this opioid

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:49.069
