00:32:59.030 --> 00:33:01.370
who has a mental illness

00:33:02.700 --> 00:33:04.729
who is acting out in some

00:33:04.730 --> 00:33:06.739
way on the street, again

00:33:06.740 --> 00:33:08.359
committing some other crime.

00:33:08.360 --> 00:33:10.399
It's not just that they

00:33:10.400 --> 00:33:12.439
are mentally ill and hurting

00:33:12.440 --> 00:33:14.179
nobody, it's that something

00:33:14.180 --> 00:33:16.369
happened. They're they're violating

00:33:16.370 --> 00:33:18.619
public safety in some way.

00:33:18.620 --> 00:33:18.634
And again, you encountered activists
who said offering treatment as an

00:33:18.634 --> 00:33:19.634
alternativeis not

00:33:27.050 --> 00:33:28.050
the right path forward.

00:33:29.120 --> 00:33:30.559
You know, in that, so there's the

00:33:30.560 --> 00:33:32.519
argument I had with the ACLU

00:33:32.520 --> 00:33:34.640
and the ACLU in California,

00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:37.609
and she says,

00:33:37.610 --> 00:33:38.959
I says, Well, what if somebody is

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:41.329
defecating you know on the sidewalk?

00:33:42.680 --> 00:33:44.269
Shouldn't they be arrested?

00:33:44.270 --> 00:33:45.679
And she said, Well, it depends on

00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:46.699
what they're doing. And she said,

00:33:46.700 --> 00:33:47.700
you know, if it's

00:33:48.770 --> 00:33:51.379
if it's a frat guy urinating

00:33:51.380 --> 00:33:52.909
on my driveway, then he should be

00:33:52.910 --> 00:33:53.910
arrested.

00:33:54.830 --> 00:33:56.659
But if it's a poor

00:33:56.660 --> 00:33:58.579
homeless person, you know,

00:33:58.580 --> 00:33:59.689
then no.

00:33:59.690 --> 00:34:00.649
And I was kind of like, Well, but

00:34:00.650 --> 00:34:01.819
then like, how are you going to get

00:34:01.820 --> 00:34:03.019
them the help that they need or

00:34:03.020 --> 00:34:04.339
like, what about enforcing the laws?

00:34:04.340 --> 00:34:05.779
I mean, it just got really twisted,

00:34:07.060 --> 00:34:08.329
but it can't. That's the that's the

00:34:08.330 --> 00:34:09.749
story of victim ideology.

00:34:09.750 --> 00:34:11.119
I mean, she just thinks there's some

00:34:11.120 --> 00:34:12.319
people that are victims and some

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:13.789
people are not. And if you're a frat

00:34:13.790 --> 00:34:14.928
guy, then you're definitely not a

00:34:14.929 --> 00:34:16.428
victim. But if you're the homeless

00:34:16.429 --> 00:34:17.718
guy, you definitely are victims.

00:34:17.719 --> 00:34:20.359
That was the way she described it.

00:34:20.360 --> 00:34:21.829
I mean, look, I think there's a

00:34:21.830 --> 00:34:23.988
concern that and I share

00:34:23.989 --> 00:34:25.879
it, which is that if you

00:34:25.880 --> 00:34:27.738
get sentenced

00:34:27.739 --> 00:34:29.149
for example, to a psychiatric

00:34:29.150 --> 00:34:31.039
hospital, you're in

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:32.479
a psychotic state and you're out of

00:34:32.480 --> 00:34:33.589
control and you're sensitive

00:34:33.590 --> 00:34:35.059
psychiatric hospital and maybe

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:37.309
you're only sentenced for 90

00:34:37.310 --> 00:34:39.209
days or six months in

00:34:39.210 --> 00:34:40.698
jail. Go into that institution and

00:34:40.699 --> 00:34:42.559
never escape and never get

00:34:42.560 --> 00:34:44.718
into kind of, you know, residential

00:34:44.719 --> 00:34:45.289
care.

00:34:45.290 --> 00:34:47.839
You know, you have some harsh words

00:34:47.840 --> 00:34:49.879
in there for thinkers like

00:34:49.880 --> 00:34:50.880
Foucau,

00:34:51.770 --> 00:34:53.779
Thomas Szasz as

00:34:53.780 --> 00:34:55.908
both of whom questioned

00:34:55.909 --> 00:34:57.319
the very underpinnings of

00:34:57.320 --> 00:34:58.549
psychiatry.

00:34:58.550 --> 00:34:59.550
And

00:35:00.620 --> 00:35:02.300
I agree that

00:35:03.770 --> 00:35:05.629
in some regards, they probably

00:35:05.630 --> 00:35:06.889
went too far.

00:35:06.890 --> 00:35:09.199
But there does seem to be

00:35:09.200 --> 00:35:11.419
the core of something that

00:35:11.420 --> 00:35:14.269
we need to be vigilant of, which is

00:35:14.270 --> 00:35:17.449
you talk about wanting to expand

00:35:17.450 --> 00:35:19.369
the scope of conservatorship, which

00:35:19.370 --> 00:35:21.739
is where basically some other entity

00:35:21.740 --> 00:35:24.079
takes control of somebodies

00:35:24.080 --> 00:35:25.519
decision making, legal decision

00:35:25.520 --> 00:35:26.520
making.

00:35:27.860 --> 00:35:29.539
We know this can be abused.

00:35:29.540 --> 00:35:31.699
Britney Spears was the big

00:35:31.700 --> 00:35:33.739
recent example of that.

00:35:33.740 --> 00:35:36.049
Is it even possible to balance this

00:35:36.050 --> 00:35:37.339
adequately?

00:35:37.340 --> 00:35:39.169
Or is it too dangerous

00:35:39.170 --> 00:35:41.449
to hand the state more

00:35:41.450 --> 00:35:43.699
and more ability to

00:35:43.700 --> 00:35:45.589
have conservatorship over

00:35:45.590 --> 00:35:47.539
people because they have

00:35:47.540 --> 00:35:49.369
a mental illness as the state

00:35:49.370 --> 00:35:50.629
defines it?

00:35:50.630 --> 00:35:51.979
Yeah. Well, let me let me address

00:35:51.980 --> 00:35:52.909
the Britney Spears thing.

00:35:52.910 --> 00:35:54.259
I actually had Britney in the book

00:35:54.260 --> 00:35:55.759
and she and I and she came out of

00:35:55.760 --> 00:35:57.349
the book towards the end because

00:35:57.350 --> 00:35:59.179
she's not, you know, the book's

00:35:59.180 --> 00:35:59.959
not about me.

00:35:59.960 --> 00:36:01.909
She's she's a rich

00:36:01.910 --> 00:36:02.779
celebrity. Right?

00:36:02.780 --> 00:36:05.119
But but we don't know

00:36:05.120 --> 00:36:07.309
that she that her conservatorship

00:36:07.310 --> 00:36:08.299
was inappropriate.

00:36:08.300 --> 00:36:10.039
We have no evidence for that

