00:37:34.970 --> 00:37:36.110
used for addiction.

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:38.689
You know, what I would say is, I

00:37:38.690 --> 00:37:40.459
mean, look, there's never of course,

00:37:40.460 --> 00:37:41.959
there's no.

00:37:41.960 --> 00:37:43.279
Of course, it's always going to be

00:37:43.280 --> 00:37:45.389
wrong in some way.

00:37:45.390 --> 00:37:46.279
There's always going to be some

00:37:46.280 --> 00:37:48.379
people that are getting are getting

00:37:48.380 --> 00:37:49.729
mistreated by too much state

00:37:49.730 --> 00:37:50.809
involvement. And there's going to be

00:37:50.810 --> 00:37:51.919
some people that aren't going to be

00:37:51.920 --> 00:37:53.509
getting enough.

00:37:53.510 --> 00:37:55.429
If you kind of go the Dutch, get

00:37:55.430 --> 00:37:57.019
as close as anybody has gotten.

00:37:57.020 --> 00:37:58.579
I'm sure you'll find people that

00:37:58.580 --> 00:38:00.349
were unjustly hospitalized and

00:38:00.350 --> 00:38:02.239
Netherlands and some people

00:38:02.240 --> 00:38:03.529
that are that needed more care and

00:38:03.530 --> 00:38:04.530
didn't get it.

00:38:05.270 --> 00:38:06.799
I will say that I think if you have

00:38:06.800 --> 00:38:08.449
the functioning care system that we

00:38:08.450 --> 00:38:10.159
don't have, then you're going to

00:38:10.160 --> 00:38:11.539
reduce that a lot.

00:38:11.540 --> 00:38:13.159
You know, we just find that in

00:38:13.160 --> 00:38:15.589
places where the care is better,

00:38:15.590 --> 00:38:17.059
there's a lot less resistance to the

00:38:17.060 --> 00:38:17.959
care.

00:38:17.960 --> 00:38:19.939
And your big picture

00:38:19.940 --> 00:38:21.949
idea for

00:38:21.950 --> 00:38:23.839
fixing the dysfunction on the

00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:25.759
streets is this agency

00:38:25.760 --> 00:38:27.649
that you call Cal Psych,

00:38:27.650 --> 00:38:29.649
which would override

00:38:29.650 --> 00:38:31.479
all the local mental health

00:38:31.480 --> 00:38:33.549
departments and funnel both those

00:38:33.550 --> 00:38:35.499
with mental illnesses and

00:38:35.500 --> 00:38:37.359
drug addictions into a

00:38:37.360 --> 00:38:38.559
centralized system.

00:38:39.580 --> 00:38:41.619
And you're a critic of what

00:38:41.620 --> 00:38:43.239
you call the results of the

00:38:43.240 --> 00:38:45.819
neoliberal model of contracting

00:38:45.820 --> 00:38:47.409
to non-profits.

00:38:47.410 --> 00:38:49.509
But my question about that is

00:38:49.510 --> 00:38:51.489
aren't more localized public

00:38:51.490 --> 00:38:53.439
private partnerships just

00:38:53.440 --> 00:38:55.959
more accountable to communities?

00:38:55.960 --> 00:38:57.789
I mean, like, why should anyone

00:38:57.790 --> 00:39:00.099
trust a system run by

00:39:00.100 --> 00:39:02.589
California state government, which,

00:39:02.590 --> 00:39:04.569
after all, did oversee

00:39:04.570 --> 00:39:06.429
the system of abuse of insane

00:39:06.430 --> 00:39:08.499
asylums of the late 19th

00:39:08.500 --> 00:39:10.419
and early 20th

00:39:10.420 --> 00:39:11.589
century?

00:39:11.590 --> 00:39:14.319
I mean, why would I expect,

00:39:14.320 --> 00:39:15.579
you know, something run out of

00:39:15.580 --> 00:39:17.709
Sacramento to be cost effective,

00:39:17.710 --> 00:39:20.139
humane or accountable?

00:39:20.140 --> 00:39:21.549
You have to work with the social

00:39:21.550 --> 00:39:22.869
worker, you know, or you have to

00:39:22.870 --> 00:39:24.339
work with a cop in this particular

00:39:24.340 --> 00:39:26.739
way. But that that needs to occur.

00:39:27.910 --> 00:39:29.079
And that's the improvement of

00:39:29.080 --> 00:39:31.299
policing in terms of psychiatry.

00:39:31.300 --> 00:39:33.039
I'm with you. I mean, there has been

00:39:33.040 --> 00:39:33.999
some people that have called for a

00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:35.829
return to big asylums.

00:39:35.830 --> 00:39:37.869
I think the evidence and basically

00:39:37.870 --> 00:39:39.849
what everybody says that has

00:39:39.850 --> 00:39:41.709
to live in one of those is that the

00:39:41.710 --> 00:39:44.289
residential care is much preferred

00:39:44.290 --> 00:39:46.209
to big old asylums.

00:39:46.210 --> 00:39:47.589
You know, you have to remember then

00:39:47.590 --> 00:39:49.029
those residential care facilities

00:39:49.030 --> 00:39:49.929
are going to have to be in people's

00:39:49.930 --> 00:39:51.299
communities, and there's usually

00:39:51.300 --> 00:39:53.469
very strong NIMBY resistance.

00:39:53.470 --> 00:39:54.789
I do think some of that solved by

00:39:54.790 --> 00:39:55.989
being able to have those residential

00:39:55.990 --> 00:39:57.879
care facilities in

00:39:57.880 --> 00:39:59.919
other parts and spread out more

00:39:59.920 --> 00:40:01.689
uniformly around the state, not all

00:40:01.690 --> 00:40:03.069
concentrated in downtown San

00:40:03.070 --> 00:40:04.239
Francisco.

00:40:04.240 --> 00:40:05.379
But I do think that's an important

00:40:05.380 --> 00:40:06.339
reform that needs to be made and

00:40:06.340 --> 00:40:07.659
probably does need a lot of federal

00:40:07.660 --> 00:40:09.939
help. The current system is

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:12.069
a kluge in the sense that

00:40:12.070 --> 00:40:14.229
you have a bunch of charities,

00:40:14.230 --> 00:40:16.029
churches, private health care

00:40:16.030 --> 00:40:17.619
providers, rehab

00:40:19.180 --> 00:40:21.789
health care providers

00:40:21.790 --> 00:40:23.619
that are paid by

00:40:23.620 --> 00:40:25.509
taxpayers at the county

00:40:25.510 --> 00:40:27.669
at the county level, mostly

00:40:27.670 --> 00:40:30.009
to provide care.

00:40:30.010 --> 00:40:31.809
Right now, they're not accountable

00:40:31.810 --> 00:40:32.769
like they.

00:40:32.770 --> 00:40:34.389
They have been struggling for

00:40:34.390 --> 00:40:35.169
decades.

00:40:35.170 --> 00:40:36.969
One of the most depressing parts of

00:40:36.970 --> 00:40:39.129
this research is reading,

00:40:39.130 --> 00:40:40.749
you know, Blue Ribbon Commission

00:40:40.750 --> 00:40:42.579
reports from the 80s and

00:40:42.580 --> 00:40:44.859
90s or early aughts

00:40:44.860 --> 00:40:45.819
talking about how there needs to be

00:40:45.820 --> 00:40:47.919
more coordination so they can't

00:40:47.920 --> 00:40:49.930
coordinate even at the local level.

00:40:52.030 --> 00:40:53.979
So part of the problem is

00:40:53.980 --> 00:40:55.089
that part of the problem is the

00:40:55.090 --> 00:40:56.319
population itself.

00:40:56.320 --> 00:40:57.969
It's especially the addict

00:40:57.970 --> 00:40:59.679
population is highly transient.

00:40:59.680 --> 00:41:01.569
I mean, transient was one of the

00:41:01.570 --> 00:41:03.879
words that was used before we

00:41:03.880 --> 00:41:05.559
settled in on homelessness on

00:41:05.560 --> 00:41:07.089
homeless, but it's a highly

00:41:07.090 --> 00:41:09.069
transient population.

00:41:09.070 --> 00:41:10.959
If it's a criminal population,

00:41:10.960 --> 00:41:12.489
so often they're often escaping

00:41:12.490 --> 00:41:14.259
local police jurisdictions that are

00:41:14.260 --> 00:41:15.519
after them.

00:41:15.520 --> 00:41:16.899
But if they're mentally ill, there's

00:41:16.900 --> 00:41:19.449
also a high level of transients.

00:41:19.450 --> 00:41:21.339
So, you know, it's just a

00:41:21.340 --> 00:41:22.569
huge problem in that sense.

00:41:22.570 --> 00:41:24.399
But the other big problem is is

00:41:24.400 --> 00:41:26.589
the is the cost and so in

00:41:26.590 --> 00:41:28.629
in San Francisco, San Francisco,

00:41:28.630 --> 00:41:30.159
Los Angeles is attracting huge

00:41:30.160 --> 00:41:32.229
numbers of addicts from around

00:41:32.230 --> 00:41:34.089
the region, the state and the

00:41:34.090 --> 00:41:35.469
country.

00:41:35.470 --> 00:41:36.999
You know, as people on the streets

00:41:37.000 --> 00:41:38.799
from Cleveland and West Virginia,

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:40.149
it's very expensive.

00:41:40.150 --> 00:41:41.799
I mean, San Francisco just can't do

00:41:41.800 --> 00:41:43.209
any services because they can't

00:41:43.210 --> 00:41:44.589
provide psychiatric and addiction

00:41:44.590 --> 00:41:47.199
care for every addict in America.

00:41:47.200 --> 00:41:48.729
It has to be able to move people

00:41:48.730 --> 00:41:50.559
places it has to build.

00:41:50.560 --> 00:41:52.689
And so you have a physical problem

00:41:52.690 --> 00:41:53.949
of we don't have enough homeless

00:41:53.950 --> 00:41:55.600
shelters, psychiatric beds,

00:41:56.620 --> 00:41:58.239
residential care in San Francisco,

00:41:58.240 --> 00:41:59.019
and you never will.

00:41:59.020 --> 00:42:00.519
And by the way, I'm arguing for a

00:42:00.520 --> 00:42:02.709
super hierarchical,

00:42:02.710 --> 00:42:05.169
transparent agency

00:42:05.170 --> 00:42:07.209
that does contracts

00:42:07.210 --> 00:42:09.129
with these different providers.

00:42:09.130 --> 00:42:10.269
So there'll still be a lot of

00:42:10.270 --> 00:42:11.409
private sector providers.

00:42:11.410 --> 00:42:12.819
I don't think you need to nationalize

00:42:12.820 --> 00:42:13.659
the entire sector.

00:42:13.660 --> 00:42:15.849
For example, the Dutch,

00:42:15.850 --> 00:42:17.439
for example, do a big subcontract

00:42:17.440 --> 00:42:18.399
with Salvation Army.

00:42:18.400 --> 00:42:19.719
Two thousand Salvation Army

00:42:19.720 --> 00:42:21.639
employees work in a contract

00:42:21.640 --> 00:42:22.509
for the Dutch government.

00:42:22.510 --> 00:42:24.849
It would be a government agency

00:42:24.850 --> 00:42:26.799
with a CEO who reports directly

00:42:26.800 --> 00:42:28.809
to the governor with like regional

00:42:28.810 --> 00:42:32.109
managers who then oversee

00:42:32.110 --> 00:42:34.329
caseworkers and contracts.

00:42:34.330 --> 00:42:35.709
And I think the power of having a

00:42:35.710 --> 00:42:37.569
new agency with new leadership is

00:42:37.570 --> 00:42:38.889
you can create a new culture, you

00:42:38.890 --> 00:42:40.859
can create an institution and

00:42:40.860 --> 00:42:43.329
it can be really high functioning

00:42:43.330 --> 00:42:44.949
if it's allowed to kind of rebuild

00:42:44.950 --> 00:42:46.599
from scratch as a kind of

00:42:46.600 --> 00:42:48.519
skunkworks, rather than having

00:42:48.520 --> 00:42:50.379
to somehow try out of reform

00:42:50.380 --> 00:42:51.939
these institutions or, God forbid,

00:42:51.940 --> 00:42:52.929
coordinate.

00:42:52.930 --> 00:42:55.209
I mean, like these are institutions

00:42:55.210 --> 00:42:57.129
that just are resistant to any

00:42:57.130 --> 00:42:58.869
amount of coordination.

00:42:58.870 --> 00:43:00.729
Is there any

00:43:00.730 --> 00:43:02.589
hope in your mind that

00:43:02.590 --> 00:43:04.299
the healing is going to begin any

00:43:04.300 --> 00:43:05.079
time soon?

00:43:05.080 --> 00:43:07.089
Are you optimistic about that?

00:43:07.090 --> 00:43:09.099
Or do you think that it

00:43:09.100 --> 00:43:11.139
still needs to get worse before

00:43:11.140 --> 00:43:12.279
it gets better?

00:43:12.280 --> 00:43:13.479
I don't think it does have to get

00:43:13.480 --> 00:43:14.829
worse before it gets better.

00:43:14.830 --> 00:43:15.609
I'm not.

00:43:15.610 --> 00:43:17.269
I've always been a proponent of

00:43:17.270 --> 00:43:19.389
immiseration theory in general,

00:43:19.390 --> 00:43:21.399
including in my in my Marxist

00:43:21.400 --> 00:43:22.839
days.

00:43:22.840 --> 00:43:24.489
I think that the whole point of

00:43:24.490 --> 00:43:26.319
political advocacy is that you can

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:27.320
raise the bottom.

00:43:28.390 --> 00:43:30.429
Of our word from addiction

00:43:30.430 --> 00:43:32.529
intervention, we are seeing

00:43:32.530 --> 00:43:34.179
a significant response in San

00:43:34.180 --> 00:43:36.069
Francisco, and I'm happy

00:43:36.070 --> 00:43:37.809
to contribute to it to the extent

00:43:37.810 --> 00:43:38.859
that we have for the last few

00:43:38.860 --> 00:43:40.479
months, we started protesting open

00:43:40.480 --> 00:43:42.039
drug dealing in May.

00:43:42.040 --> 00:43:43.479
We've organized the California Peace

00:43:43.480 --> 00:43:45.279
Coalition with parents of street

00:43:45.280 --> 00:43:46.779
addicts, parents of kids killed by

00:43:46.780 --> 00:43:48.339
fentanyl with recovering addicts and

00:43:48.340 --> 00:43:49.989
community leaders.

00:43:49.990 --> 00:43:52.509
It is having an impact.

00:43:52.510 --> 00:43:54.399
Just, you know, the book has had a

00:43:54.400 --> 00:43:56.829
big impact in San Francisco.

00:43:56.830 --> 00:43:58.659
The mayor announced

00:43:58.660 --> 00:44:00.579
a crackdown on open drug dealing

00:44:00.580 --> 00:44:02.469
in mid-December.

00:44:02.470 --> 00:44:04.809
She immediately experienced pushback

00:44:04.810 --> 00:44:05.769
from the San Francisco Board of

00:44:05.770 --> 00:44:06.909
Supervisors.

00:44:06.910 --> 00:44:08.829
She's now getting bureaucratic

00:44:08.830 --> 00:44:10.839
pushback, but she's pushing back

00:44:10.840 --> 00:44:12.249
against the pushback.

00:44:12.250 --> 00:44:13.299
You know, I get a lot of calls from

00:44:13.300 --> 00:44:14.889
people all the time with different

00:44:14.890 --> 00:44:17.319
kind of views of what's going on.

00:44:17.320 --> 00:44:19.419
But I do have hope.

00:44:19.420 --> 00:44:21.429
And it did. It did help that Fox

00:44:21.430 --> 00:44:23.139
News and the conservative national

00:44:23.140 --> 00:44:24.849
news media were pounding away on San

00:44:24.850 --> 00:44:26.469
Francisco. I mean, the San Francisco

00:44:26.470 --> 00:44:28.359
brand is in deep

00:44:28.360 --> 00:44:30.249
trouble and it will be for many

00:44:30.250 --> 00:44:31.749
years to come until they fixed it.

00:44:31.750 --> 00:44:33.339
I mean, we hear reports of people

00:44:33.340 --> 00:44:35.169
from Reykjavik and from Thailand who

00:44:35.170 --> 00:44:36.729
are like, Yeah, we were just talking

00:44:36.730 --> 00:44:38.499
about because, you know, the bad the

00:44:38.500 --> 00:44:40.539
horrible situation in San Francisco

00:44:40.540 --> 00:44:42.549
has made international news.

00:44:42.550 --> 00:44:44.349
So I do think we have to keep the

00:44:44.350 --> 00:44:46.179
pressure on, and I think we need

00:44:46.180 --> 00:44:47.649
to praise the politicians from

00:44:47.650 --> 00:44:48.849
whatever party they're coming from

00:44:48.850 --> 00:44:50.049
when they move in our direction,

00:44:50.050 --> 00:44:51.969
even if it's not very far.

00:44:51.970 --> 00:44:53.349
Are you bothered by being

00:44:53.350 --> 00:44:55.359
characterized as kind

00:44:55.360 --> 00:44:57.429
of a tool of the right wing now?

00:44:57.430 --> 00:44:59.379
I've read lots of criticisms

00:44:59.380 --> 00:45:00.759
along those lines.

00:45:00.760 --> 00:45:01.929
We started with the title of your

00:45:01.930 --> 00:45:03.849
book that kind of plays into that

00:45:03.850 --> 00:45:04.599
somewhat.

00:45:04.600 --> 00:45:05.799
I mean, nobody likes being

00:45:05.800 --> 00:45:06.800
criticized.

00:45:09.280 --> 00:45:11.169
That said, I think it bothers me

00:45:11.170 --> 00:45:14.379
less now than it ever has.

00:45:14.380 --> 00:45:16.599
Yeah, I mean, I just retweeted

00:45:16.600 --> 00:45:18.519
a friend of mine who talked

00:45:18.520 --> 00:45:20.379
about how, you know, it's like, you

00:45:20.380 --> 00:45:22.269
see, I don't get invited to go on

00:45:22.270 --> 00:45:24.489
CNN or MSNBC.

00:45:24.490 --> 00:45:26.019
My last book was ignored by the

00:45:26.020 --> 00:45:26.829
Times.

00:45:26.830 --> 00:45:28.779
This book was was was reviewed,

00:45:28.780 --> 00:45:30.439
and an attack from the reviewers

00:45:30.440 --> 00:45:32.289
lied about the book, which is

00:45:32.290 --> 00:45:33.999
to be expected.

00:45:34.000 --> 00:45:37.209
But it also got an Editors Choice

00:45:37.210 --> 00:45:39.249
mentioned in the year end review

00:45:39.250 --> 00:45:41.229
for the Times I,

00:45:41.230 --> 00:45:43.509
after the mayor of San Francisco,

00:45:43.510 --> 00:45:44.649
announced a crackdown.

00:45:44.650 --> 00:45:46.179
I was invited to finally give a

00:45:46.180 --> 00:45:47.649
speech at the Commonwealth Club,

00:45:47.650 --> 00:45:50.619
which is sort of our liberal,

00:45:50.620 --> 00:45:52.599
you know, high society

00:45:52.600 --> 00:45:54.549
club. So

00:45:54.550 --> 00:45:57.279
there is a thawing occurring.

00:45:57.280 --> 00:45:59.739
I mean, generally, I get attacked

00:45:59.740 --> 00:46:01.689
at the exact same moment that

00:46:01.690 --> 00:46:04.179
my work is having some impact, so

00:46:04.180 --> 00:46:06.099
I kind of remind myself of

00:46:06.100 --> 00:46:07.100
that.

00:46:07.570 --> 00:46:09.159
You know, and I'm I'm not a right

00:46:09.160 --> 00:46:11.199
winger, and I'm not just

00:46:11.200 --> 00:46:12.939
demanding, you know, a return to the

00:46:12.940 --> 00:46:14.409
drug war, and I'm not just wrapping

00:46:14.410 --> 00:46:15.699
things in language, I'm actually

00:46:15.700 --> 00:46:18.039
proposing a pretty substantive

00:46:18.040 --> 00:46:19.449
new social program.

00:46:19.450 --> 00:46:21.699
Apocalypse Never was last year

00:46:21.700 --> 00:46:23.079
and that's my book on the environment

00:46:23.080 --> 00:46:25.389
and it was just so taboo.

00:46:25.390 --> 00:46:27.279
And so I was just such

00:46:27.280 --> 00:46:30.459
persona non grata really until now.

00:46:30.460 --> 00:46:31.599
Where it is starting to be taken a

00:46:31.600 --> 00:46:32.619
little bit more seriously by

00:46:32.620 --> 00:46:33.849
liberals, still not as much, I

00:46:33.850 --> 00:46:34.989
think, as San Fransicko, but I think

00:46:34.990 --> 00:46:37.179
San Fransicko, the ideas

00:46:37.180 --> 00:46:39.039
in it are maybe

00:46:39.040 --> 00:46:40.599
paradoxically given the title, but

00:46:40.600 --> 00:46:41.739
they're actually there are more

00:46:41.740 --> 00:46:43.449
palatable to people, to liberal

00:46:43.450 --> 00:46:45.399
people when they really consider it.

00:46:45.400 --> 00:46:47.799
And then so you know, you've got to

00:46:47.800 --> 00:46:48.639
the other day.

00:46:48.640 --> 00:46:50.109
My philosophy is sort of like if you

00:46:50.110 --> 00:46:52.329
try too hard to

00:46:52.330 --> 00:46:54.129
make your ideas palatable, then

00:46:54.130 --> 00:46:56.229
you're at risk of corrupting

00:46:56.230 --> 00:46:57.309
the ideas themselves.

00:46:57.310 --> 00:46:58.599
And so you have to have some faith

00:46:58.600 --> 00:47:00.939
in that that the times

00:47:00.940 --> 00:47:02.889
will change to catch up with the

00:47:02.890 --> 00:47:03.890
ideas.

